A||||9||ezsurf||EGR Tube for 2.8
Z||000000||ezsurf||05-23-99||09:50 PM||||<br>I'm probably going to need another EGR tube for my 2.8.  I doubt the old one can be repaired.  A new one from GM costs $75.00.  IRM makes one out of flex stainless steel that's not suposed to break for $90.00.  This is awful high for a simple tube.  Anyone know where to get a cheaper fix?  Also, does anyone know how vital a system this is?  I was considering making some plates and capping the system off.  I have a feeling that this is for nothing more than emissions.  Anyone have any expertiese?
Z||000001||theogre||05-24-99||08:33 AM||||it's primarily an emissions device, but it's operated by the ECM in the v6.<p>performance wise it doesn't matter much most of the time. you need  to try it and see. If you get an increase in knock/ping then reconect it. <p>Check your state emmisions laws... Some states are getting really nasty about any change to the emmisions systems.
Z||000002||Carrolles||05-24-99||09:10 AM||||I know of people who have had their old egr tubes welded back together.  I believe thay only paid around $10.  It's not hard for someone who is familiar with stainless steel welding.<p>I believe the online service guide mentions that a camaro tube will fit with a bit of modification on one of the flanges
Z||000003||ezsurf||05-24-99||07:04 PM||||Thanks for the input. I was told by one weld shop that it couldn't be fixed. I'm going to try one other before I give up. If it can't be welded, I'm going to try an exhaust repair kit and see if I can make that work. If that doesn't work I'll probably just cap it. One thing that sticks in my mind is the large amount of carbon buildup in the intake manifold. I've got to believe the EGR system contributed largely to this.  Where else can carbon build up in the intake come from?  This engine is port fuel injected.  There's no air/fuel mixture in the intake. Carbon build up lined the entire intake, all the way out to the very front of the butterfly valve. I've never seen so much carbon build up. The butterfly valve was the only piece small enough for me to clean. Everything else had to go to the machine shop. If the EGR system didn't cause the build up, then I don't know where it could have come from, unless the previous owner was running it on coal. Anyone have any thoughts?
Z||000004||Eric||05-24-99||07:56 PM||||I have the same thing going on in my intake.  Seems like this carbon buildup could cause problems.
Z||000005||HF6582||05-25-99||08:18 AM||||i have the egr system removed on my car, and ihave no problems.<br>
Z||000006||Carrolles||05-27-99||06:50 AM||||I found this on the Fiero Secrets Page.<p>Hope this helps,<br>Carroll<br>---------------------------------------------------------<p>EGR Tube Substitute <p>A friend of mine recently acquired a V-6 Fiero with (of course) a cracked EGR tube. Rather than replace it with another Fiero tube (which he couldn't find in a local salvage yard), he scrounged a tube off a 2.8 Firebird. The bolts on the manifold flange were spaced a bit differently so he used his grinder and made the bolt holes into slots which allowed the tube to bolt up to the Fiero manifold. The only thing is... the "new" tube doesn't have the little heat blanket. Dunno if that's any real problem or not. Personally, I'd rather have the blanket just because any Fiero people who look under there will know it's not "correct". <p>Not bad for a $1.00 fix.... <br>-Al Hunt <br>The opinions expressed in this article are those of the author. <br>They also happen to be right. <br>Posted from Rancho Fiero 
Z||000007||ezsurf||06-01-99||07:07 PM||||I have a possible explanation for the carbon build up in my engine.  After talking with a mechanic he said it was most likely oil blow by past the valves.  The EGR system probably helped, but was most likely the valves because the build up was wet with oil.  Exhaust gas wouldn't be wet like oil.
Z||000008||LEE498||06-01-99||07:28 PM||||HF6582 how did you go about removing the egr system on the v6? did you notice an increase in power?
Z||000009||ezsurf||06-04-99||06:39 AM||||HF6582,<br>I would also like to know how you by-passed the EGR system.  Did you remove the vacuum valve and cap it, or did you simply cap the EGR tube ports?  I'm not concerned that eliminating ths system will adversely effect how the engine runs.  I just want the system sealed properly with no exhaust leaks.
